#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf29db1b-f47b-53de-b31d-e4da1d0a9e5f" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Triple Integral as a Mixed or Repeated Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8572ac66-a145-5e45-ac8a-d7dbd1dcfe84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347067} {:answer/id #uuid "7828a55f-4034-56a0-8344-96fd05e719a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347068} {:answer/id #uuid "7a94b854-2c4b-5bb7-b887-6624a91d6500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+2xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347069} {:answer/id #uuid "8539bd20-dca1-5322-91d9-59a00c03affd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347070} {:answer/id #uuid "1f3bb41f-4a90-5a2a-b836-7a4d3713f68c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8539bd20-dca1-5322-91d9-59a00c03affd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347070} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f9100e-094b-574a-ba60-39da8e7739f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7395b4b7-89c8-50fe-abf9-2def2b92024d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161729" :question/problem "$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx\n$$\n\nThe triple integral above is defined over the type III region given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 2x,\\,2\\le y\\le 2xz}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a\\times b?$" :question/worked-solution "A type III region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {u}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063571/assets/0603bed12c6af74e46544d8bebc52362aed95a9a5ad7418ea4faaf228f3b5241.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type III region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,z)=2,\\,{u}_{2}(x,z)=2xz.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{2}^{2xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}[{\\int }_{2}^{2xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dz\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{\\int }_{2}^{2xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na\\times b=(0)\\times (2x)=0.\n$$"}]}
